system "ALA-LC:chu-Cyrl:Latn:2022" {

metadata {
  authority_id alalc
  id 2022
  language iso-639-2:chu
  source_script Cyrl
  destination_script Latn
  name Romanization Table -- Church Slavic (2022)
  url https://www.loc.gov/catdir/cpso/romanization/church-slavic.pdf
  creation_date 2022
  description {
    ALA-LC romanization table for Church Slavic, 2022 version
    (earlier versions 2011 and 1997). Covers the full Church Slavonic
    alphabet including the yuses, ksi, psi, fita, izhitsa and the two uk
    forms, with the letters' numeric values annotated. Transcribed from
    the digitized table (pages 1-2).
  }

  notes {
    note "Diacritical signs: accents and aspiration marks are ignored in romanization, including the acute accent, the grave accent, the aspiration mark, and combinations thereof. The letters ї and ѷ are sometimes printed with two dots (or marks): when no other accent appears the dots have no particular significance and are ignored; when the dots (or other diacritical marks) indicate that the letter is a vowel, the letter is romanized ẏ. The symbol indicating a short i (breve) is taken into account in romanization, resulting in ĭ."
    note "Abbreviations: abbreviated words are transcribed in full, without the use of brackets. The most common symbol for abbreviation is the titlo; omitted letters may be placed above other letters, and the symbol above a letter indicates the omission of ъ or ь."
    note "Numerals: represented in Church Slavic by letters (as shown in the romanization table) with the addition of the signs ҂, U+20DD, U+0488 and U+0489; an added titlo indicates a basic numeral (в҃ = 2), and higher numerals: ҂в҃ = 2,000, в U+20DD = 20,000, в U+0488 = 200,000, в U+0489 = 2,000,000; the figure 1913 is written ҂аці҃г. This map renders the letters, not the numeral values."
    note "Dates: Church Slavic books dated by the Mundane Era of Constantinople convert to the Christian Era by subtracting 5508; prior to 1700 the mundane year began on September 1st, so two years are given by subtracting 5509 (September through December) and 5508 (January through August); thus 7203 in the Mundane Era is 1694/95 in the Christian Era."
    note "The iotated letters ѥ, ю, ꙗ, Ѩ and Ѭ are romanized Ī plus tie plus the vowel (Ī͡e, Ī͡u, Ī͡a, Ī͡ę, Ī͡ǫ), mirroring the table's romanization of і as Ī; the digraph-letter Ѿ (ot) is romanized Ō plus tie plus t (ō͡t); the digraph-letter pairs Ѯ and Ѱ are romanized k͡s and p͡s with the tie."
    note "The table lists two uk forms, both with numeric value 400: the monograph uk Ꙋ is romanized U and the digraph uk Ѹ is romanized Ū. Both forms are encoded."
    note "The table's row for ћ (used for the soft g in Serbian-printed Church Slavic) gives ǵ, distinct from the Serbian table's đ."
    note "Izhitsa ѷ is romanized ẏ (Y with dot above); the table notes the alternative rendering v̇ when the letter is used without diacritical marks. Only the vowel rendering ẏ is encoded, as the alternative cannot be resolved without reading context."
    note "Source erratum: the lower-case row for и prints the romanization as uppercase I. Encoded as i for case parity; flagged in the pull request."
    note "Numeric-value column of the source table: А=1, В=2, Г=3, Д=4, Є=5, Ѕ=6, З=7, И=8, І=10, К=20, Л=30, М=40, Н=50, О=70, П=80, Р=100, С=200, Т=300, both uk forms=400, Ф=500, Х=600, Ѯ=60, Ѱ=700, Ѡ and Ѿ=800, Ѧ=900, Ѳ=9, Ѷ=400."
    note "Transcribed visually from the scanned table pages; the romanization cells for Ѥ, Ѿ, Ц, Ы, Ю, Ꙗ, Ѯ, Ѱ, Ѩ and Ѭ are embedded raster images in the source PDF and were verified at magnification from the extracted image rasters."
  }
}

tests {
  # every table row, upper and lower (the document has no example words
  # beyond the table itself)
   "А" ->  "A"
   "а" ->  "a"
   "Б" ->  "B"
